{
 "clinical_assessment": "The patient has Brugada syndrome, a genetic cardiac channelopathy characterized by sodium-channel dysfunction. Loperamide, a peripherally acting opioid agonist, can cause dose-dependent QT-interval prolongation and torsades de pointes. The patient is exceeding the maximum recommended dose (16 mg/day) for persistent diarrhea, creating a hazard of excessive loperamide exposure.",
 "drug_rule": "Loperamide blocks the M-type potassium channel in the ileocecal valve, reducing peristalsis and slowing gastrointestinal transit. At supratherapeutic doses, it can inhibit the delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr), prolonging the QT interval and increasing the risk of ventricular arrhythmias, particularly in patients with pre-existing cardiac structural or electrophysiological abnormalities such as Brugada syndrome.",
